{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Slay ye every one his men</b> — Every single one of the judges of Israel would slay two, and the judges of Israel were seventy-eight thousand, as is stated in Sanhedrin (18a); this is the language of Rashi. But I do not understand this, for it would mean that those who cleaved to Baal Peor who were condemned were numerous — fifteen ribbo (a hundred and fifty thousand), a quarter of Israel — Heaven forbid! And capital cases are not judged by a single judge but by a Sanhedrin of twenty-three, and even so they would be numerous, and the deficiency in the count of the census (Numbers 26:51) is not all that great. And all who cleaved died, as it is written: \"for all the men that followed Baal Peor, HaShem your Elohim has destroyed them from among you\" (Deuteronomy 4:3).\n\nRather, the meaning of <b>every one his men</b> is that the judges should slay all who cleaved — each court should judge its tribe and its thousand, as it is written: \"So I took the heads of your tribes, men of wisdom and understanding, and made them heads over you, captains of thousands and captains of hundreds and officers for your tribes\" (Deuteronomy 1:15).\n\nAfterward I found in the Gemara Yerushalmi in the chapter Cheilek (Sanhedrin 10:2) as follows: And how many were the judges of Israel? Seventy-eight thousand and six hundred. He said to them: Each one of you shall kill two. It is found that the slain were fifteen ribbo and seven thousand and two hundred. If so, we would say according to their opinion that Israel multiplied from census to census very much indeed.\n\nAnd what seems correct in my eyes regarding the matter of the pericope is this: At first it was stated, \"And Israel joined himself to Baal Peor, and the anger of HaShem was kindled against Israel\" (Numbers 25:3), for the wrath went forth from before HaShem; the plague had begun. And HaShem in His mercy said to Moses that the judges should judge and hang those who cleaved, so that the wrath would not consume the righteous along with the wicked. And Moses so commanded the judges, and when all the congregation assembled at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting to do according to the word of Moses — while the plague was still raging among them — behold, this Simeonite prince acted brazenly and brought the Midianite woman near to his brethren, to rebel against Moses and the judges and to do so publicly, for he was a prince and a great man, with many supporting him; or as our Sages said (Sanhedrin 82a): his tribe said to him, \"We are being condemned to death and you remain silent?\" And behold, Moses and the judges began to weep, and Pinchas stood up and prayed, and the plague was stayed. And not a single one of all the people was judged by the hand of the judges, for HaShem had said: \"Hang them up... that the fierce anger of HaShem may turn away from Israel\" (Numbers 25:4) — and His anger had already turned back. And therefore Scripture did not mention, \"And the judges of Israel did so.\"\n\nAnd it is possible that the intent of the cited aggadah is to say that the slain, according to the commandment, would have been more than fifteen ribbo, to say that the sinners were many, but the matter of Pinchas spared them. And behold, of those who cleaved, some remained, and the Holy One, blessed be He, destroyed them afterward before they crossed the Jordan. This is what is written: \"For all the men that followed Baal Peor, HaShem your Elohim has destroyed them from among you. But you that did cleave to HaShem your Elohim are alive every one of you this day\" (Deuteronomy 4:3–4)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The verse quotation from Numbers 25:4 in the latter section includes only a partial phrase; Ramban cites the beginning of the verse, 'והוקע אותם' ('hang them up'), and then the latter clause, 'ושב חרון אף ה׳ מישראל' (rendered here as it appears in the Hebrew text with the slight orthographic variant חרון/חרי)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:52.517394914Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}